==English==
{{was wotd|2006|February|27}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{qualifier|plural of anaphora}} [[anaphoras]], [[anaphors]]
* {{qualifier|plural of anaphor}} [[anaphors]]

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|grc|en}} {{m|grc|ἀναφορά||a carrying back}}, from {{m|grc|ἀνά||up}} + {{m|grc|φέρω||I carry}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ænəˈfɔɹə/|/ənˈæfəɹə/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|anaphors|anaphora}}
{{examples-right|sense=rhetoric|examples=“Mad world! Mad kings! Mad composition!” - Shakespeare}}
{{examples-right|sense=expression referring to a preceding expression|examples=That's John's car. <u>He</u> [referring to "John"] won't want to see you sitting on <u>it</u> [referring to the car].<br>John had a drink. So <u>did</u> [referring to "had a drink"] Mark.<br>John had been feeling rather dehydrated. Mark was even more <u>so</u> [referring to "dehydrated"].}}

# {{lb|en|rhetoric}} The [[repetition]] of a [[phrase]] at the beginning of phrases, [[sentence]]s, or [[verse]]s, used for [[emphasis]].
# {{lb|en|linguistics}} An [[expression]] that can [[refer]] to virtually any referent, the specific referent being defined by context.
# {{lb|en|linguistics}} An expression that refers to a preceding expression.
# {{lb|en|Christianity}} The most solemn part of the [[Divine Liturgy]] or the [[Mass]] during which the [[offering]]s of bread and wine are [[consecrate]]d

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|anaphoric}}

====Usage notes====
* In linguistics, the terms {{m|en|anaphor}} and {{m|en||anaphora}} are sometimes used interchangeably, although in some theories, a distinction is made between them. See [[w:Anaphora (linguistics)|the Wikipedia article]].

